# ...but Google's attestation roots are PUBLIC trust anchors, not credentials,
# and `*.pem` above was silently swallowing them. The consequence was not a
# quiet one: `loadRoots()` fails fast by design, so the file never reaching the
# image took the whole service down on deploy (Render, 2026-08-06 — "ENOENT:
# /app/backend/data/google-attestation-roots.pem", exit status 1).
#
# The rule above is kept exactly as it is: a blanket `*.pem` ban is the right
# default when the same tree holds signing material, and weakening it to fix one
# public file would trade a deploy failure for a leaked-key risk. This is a
# single-file exception instead, and it is only correct while the file stays what
# it claims to be — PUBLIC certificates. Verified before un-ignoring: the file
# contains 2 CERTIFICATE blocks and no PRIVATE KEY block. Re-check that before
# adding any further exception here.
!backend/data/google-attestation-roots.pem

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Photographs, screenshots, screen recordings — DENY BY DEFAULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is an evidence app, so ordinary development means pulling real captures,
# screenshots and exported bundles off a real handset — and every one of those is
# a photograph of someone's actual surroundings, carrying full-precision GPS and
# a device serial in EXIF. Two properties make this worth a blanket rule rather
# than care:
#
#   1. It is irreversible. `git rm` in a later commit leaves the photo in
#      history, on every clone, fork and mirror. There is no fixing it after a
#      push, only rewriting history everyone else has already pulled.
#   2. It is silent. A stray `adb pull` into the repo root is one line in
#      `git status` among many, and `git add -A` takes it without comment.
#
# Committed images are therefore listed by EXACT PATH below, never by folder
# glob. `androidTest/assets/*.jpg` would silently re-admit anything later dropped
# in that folder, which is the accident this section exists to prevent. Adding a
# fixture is meant to cost one deliberate line here — and that line is the place
# to record that you checked what is inside it.
#
# The bracket syntax is not decoration. gitignore patterns are case-SENSITIVE
# (core.ignorecase is false on Linux), and a plain `*.jpg` does not match
# `IMG_4821.JPG` — which is what iPhones, DSLRs and most card readers actually
# write. `*.heic` missing `.HEIC` was a live hole in this section when it was
# first written, caught only by testing the rules against realistic filenames.
# `[jJ][pP][gG]` covers every casing, including mixed, with one pattern.
*.[jJ][pP][gG]
*.[jJ][pP][eE][gG]
*.[pP][nN][gG]
*.[gG][iI][fF]
*.[bB][mM][pP]
*.[wW][eE][bB][pP]
*.[aA][vV][iI][fF]
*.[tT][iI][fF]
*.[tT][iI][fF][fF]
*.[hH][eE][iI][cC]
*.[hH][eE][iI][fF]
# Raw formats: phones increasingly write these alongside the JPEG, and they are
# larger, un-viewable in a diff, and carry the same EXIF.
*.[dD][nN][gG]
*.[cC][rR]2
*.[nN][eE][fF]
*.[aA][rR][wW]
*.[rR][aA][fF]
*.[oO][rR][fF]
# Video: screen recordings of the capture flow, and anything the camera shot.
*.[mM][pP]4
*.[mM][oO][vV]
*.[aA][vV][iI]
*.[mM][kK][vV]
*.[wW][eE][bB][mM]
*.3[gG][pP]

# Exported evidence bundles: a ZIP whose entire purpose is to carry the
# photograph and its signed package together. Pulled off the device constantly
# while testing the backup path. No .zip is tracked in this repo (the Gradle
# wrapper is a .jar and is unaffected).
*.[zZ][iI][pP]

# The photo's sidecar. Captures are stored as `<eventId>.jpg` + `<eventId>.json`
# where eventId is a `UUID.randomUUID()` (CaptureCoordinator), so the same
# `adb pull` that grabs a photograph grabs a proof package holding FULL-precision
# GPS — arguably the more sensitive half, and the half that does not look like an
# image in `git status`.
#
# The pattern matches the three fixed 4-hex-digit groups of a UUID rather than
# `*-*-*-*-*.json`, which was tried first and hit the deliberate, committed
# `docs/evidence/2026-07-24-after-clockbase-fix.json`. Lowercase only is correct:
# Java's UUID.toString() is lowercase by definition. Curated evidence samples with
# human-readable names are unaffected, which is the intended split — a file someone
# named on purpose is reviewed, a UUID-named one fell off a device.
*-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-*.json

# --- Allowed: synthetic or public test fixtures ----------------------------
